WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.153
You have probably done this yourself.

2
00:00:02.173 --> 00:00:05.643
You work out every piece of a hard problem,
and still get the answer wrong,

3
00:00:05.663 --> 00:00:08.620
because one piece never makes it into the
final step.

4
00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:12.950
Language models do the same, even when we hand
them the answer to every step.

5
00:00:12.970 --> 00:00:18.415
This is OracleLadder, our NeurIPS 2026 paper
on where LLM math reasoning breaks.

6
00:00:19.165 --> 00:00:20.875
Here is a short geometry problem.

7
00:00:20.895 --> 00:00:23.406
Line p is y = 2x − 1.

8
00:00:23.426 --> 00:00:28.897
Through the point (2, 3) on p, draw the line q
perpendicular to p.

9
00:00:28.917 --> 00:00:34.118
Find the area of the convex quadrilateral that
p, q, and the two axes enclose.

10
00:00:34.722 --> 00:00:37.968
Qwen3-8B, a 2025 model, with thinking turned
off,

11
00:00:37.988 --> 00:00:40.388
gets it wrong in all eight attempts.

12
00:00:40.991 --> 00:00:42.361
But which part did it fail?

13
00:00:42.965 --> 00:00:46.965
The answer decides what to fix, whether you
train reasoning models,

14
00:00:46.985 --> 00:00:49.405
compare them, or build benchmarks.

15
00:00:49.425 --> 00:00:54.212
First, the milestone test. Can the model solve
each milestone on its own?

16
00:00:54.232 --> 00:00:59.143
Qwen3 solves milestone 1 in 8 of 8 attempts,
and milestone 2 in 8 of 8.

17
00:00:59.839 --> 00:01:01.118
It passes.

18
00:01:01.138 --> 00:01:04.079
Then we climb a ladder of help on the full
problem.

19
00:01:04.099 --> 00:01:06.958
Rung 1: no help. 0 of 8.

20
00:01:06.978 --> 00:01:10.391
Rung 2: we add the roadmap. 0 of 8.

21
00:01:11.204 --> 00:01:17.795
Rung 3: the roadmap, plus both milestone
answers. Still 0 of 8.

22
00:01:17.815 --> 00:01:20.001
It can solve each step alone.

23
00:01:20.021 --> 00:01:22.230
It is handed the answer to every milestone.

24
00:01:22.250 --> 00:01:23.821
It still cannot finish.

25
00:01:23.841 --> 00:01:28.039
We call this a composition gap: every
milestone passes,

26
00:01:28.059 --> 00:01:31.415
yet no rung of help solves the problem.

27
00:01:31.435 --> 00:01:35.618
We ran OracleLadder on 354 NuminaMath
problems,

28
00:01:35.638 --> 00:01:42.119
and six open models from 2024 and 2025, from
8B to 671B parameters.

29
00:01:42.139 --> 00:01:47.025
For every model, the composition gap is the
largest of the five gaps,

30
00:01:47.045 --> 00:01:49.399
covering 33–48% of problems.

31
00:01:49.419 --> 00:01:52.913
Our verifier is strict, so some of these are
grading errors.

32
00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:58.586
After removing problems that an LLM review
flags as grading errors,

33
00:01:58.606 --> 00:02:00.321
it still covers 24–37%.

34
00:02:01.017 --> 00:02:04.080
What remains mixes roadmaps that miss a step,

35
00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:07.937
with real failures to combine, like the one we
just saw.

